WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.630
Have you ever bathed under a light
that creates the glow of a full moon,

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.008
sculpted beaches into miniature mountains

00:00:12.008 --> 00:00:14.970
or breathed in the aroma
of Earth’s first trees?

00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:20.433
These are some of the artworks I’ve made
to come to an understanding of deep time.

00:00:20.892 --> 00:00:22.143
But what is deep time,

00:00:22.143 --> 00:00:24.521
and why does it matter to us all?

00:00:25.146 --> 00:00:27.482
The term describes
the history of the Earth

00:00:27.482 --> 00:00:30.068
over a time span of millions of years.

00:00:30.652 --> 00:00:34.781
My first encounter with deep time
came after I’d finished my studies,

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:35.782
had no job

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:37.075
and didn’t know what to do.

00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:41.621
So I took off to work as a chambermaid
in the remote north of Iceland.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.833
I was tilted on my axis

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.294
and began a series of explorations

00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:50.505
to try to figure out
how to tell the story of deep time.

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:53.592
This has been the quest of my work,

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:55.802
and it’s taken me through the cosmos,

00:00:55.802 --> 00:00:58.346
the geological strata of the Earth,

00:00:58.346 --> 00:01:01.308
to encounters with
the earliest forms of life.

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:05.437
In Iceland I realized
that we live on a planet.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:09.816
By opening my eyes
to the primordial landscape,

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:13.361
I started to understand
we’re not born out of nothing.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.865
The sea, the sky, the Earth, the air:

00:01:16.865 --> 00:01:18.742
we’re made of the same stuff,

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:19.826
we coexist.

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:22.203
In just my lifetime,

00:01:22.203 --> 00:01:25.081
we humans have become a geological force.

00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:29.544
We’ve caused glaciers
around the world to melt entirely.

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:34.924
I wanted to bring these distant landscapes
closer to our lives in a visceral way.

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.636
So of course,
I set up a phone line.

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:40.180
People everywhere could dial a number

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.600
and listen live to the sound
of a glacier melting.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.727
(Ice cracks)

00:01:45.935 --> 00:01:49.022
It was an elegy
to disappearing landscape.

00:01:49.481 --> 00:01:53.109
(Glacier melts)

00:01:53.401 --> 00:01:56.529
Can we connect to deep time
via sensory experience?

00:01:57.197 --> 00:01:58.948
This artwork, called “Totality,”

00:01:58.948 --> 00:02:03.578
brings together nearly every solar eclipse
documented by humankind

00:02:03.953 --> 00:02:05.622
in a mirror ball.

00:02:05.622 --> 00:02:09.834
Over 10,000 images reflect
the progression of a solar eclipse.

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:11.753
Their light surrounds us,

00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:14.297
and we can feel mesmerized.

00:02:14.839 --> 00:02:15.965
This led me to wonder:

00:02:15.965 --> 00:02:17.801
What is the color of deep time?

00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:20.720
I mapped the colors
of the entire universe,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.723
from its very beginnings
to its potential ends,

00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:27.435
discovering the pale blue hues
of the first stars

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:29.938
to the dark maroons of the last light.

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:37.612
Every single atom in our bodies was formed
in a star billions of years ago.

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:40.865
The remnants of stars make up all of us.

00:02:41.991 --> 00:02:45.578
My vision was to gather
every dying star across the universe

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:46.663
and make a map.

00:02:47.122 --> 00:02:48.331
The result:

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:51.376
over 27,000 supernova,

00:02:51.376 --> 00:02:52.585
stellar black holes

00:02:52.585 --> 00:02:56.548
and gamma-ray bursts
shimmer as pinpoints of aluminum.

00:02:57.132 --> 00:02:59.884
What you see is a graveyard of stars,

00:02:59.884 --> 00:03:01.928
yet it alludes to life.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.434
The journey we humans have been on
to arrive right here

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:09.769
is nothing short of a miracle.

00:03:10.353 --> 00:03:15.608
“Fossil Necklace” is a string of worlds
made of the material of life itself.

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:18.945
Each bead is carved from ancient fossils

00:03:18.945 --> 00:03:21.614
and strung geological epoch by epoch.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.784
The beads chart major moments
in Earth’s history.

00:03:25.201 --> 00:03:27.036
The first single-celled life,

00:03:27.036 --> 00:03:28.496
the first flowers,

00:03:28.496 --> 00:03:30.290
the first creatures to see

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.374
and to fly.

00:03:31.958 --> 00:03:35.086
“Fossil Necklace” speaks
to our long continuum.

00:03:37.088 --> 00:03:39.090
Every day we walk past trees,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.843
but do we stop to think
that they are our cousins?

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:45.472
We share so many of our genes with trees,

00:03:45.472 --> 00:03:47.640
and forests give us our breath.

00:03:48.183 --> 00:03:49.684
I wanted to honor them

00:03:49.684 --> 00:03:53.688
by creating a sculpture
made of every tree type on Earth.

00:03:54.314 --> 00:03:55.690
This is “Hollow.”

00:03:56.441 --> 00:03:58.943
Designed with architects Zeller &amp; Moye,

00:03:58.943 --> 00:04:02.405
it brings together
over 10,000 tree species

00:04:02.405 --> 00:04:04.115
spanning millions of years.

00:04:04.741 --> 00:04:08.453
We were donated wood samples
from almost every country:

00:04:08.453 --> 00:04:09.871
the Indian Banyan,

00:04:09.871 --> 00:04:10.872
the White Mulberry,

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:12.373
the Dawn Redwood

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:14.959
and twigs from sacred forests around us.

00:04:15.543 --> 00:04:17.378
Looking upwards into the light,

00:04:17.378 --> 00:04:20.215
we see the most threatened
tree species of now.

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:23.426
More recently in my practice,

00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:28.056
I’ve come to believe that looking forward
is just as important as looking back.

00:04:28.932 --> 00:04:31.184
How do we speak to unborn people?

00:04:31.434 --> 00:04:33.895
How do we build a bridge across time?

00:04:34.646 --> 00:04:38.858
I thought the world's literary voices
may be the best at doing that job,

00:04:38.858 --> 00:04:41.319
so I proposed a library of the future:

00:04:41.319 --> 00:04:42.320
a forest,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.321
a room,

00:04:43.321 --> 00:04:45.615
100 authors and 100 years.

00:04:46.241 --> 00:04:48.368
It started in 2014.

00:04:48.743 --> 00:04:50.495
On the outskirts of Oslo,

00:04:50.495 --> 00:04:52.956
we planted a thousand baby Spruce trees.

00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:54.791
When they’re fully grown,

00:04:54.791 --> 00:04:56.876
they’re going to be cut
and made into paper.

00:04:57.252 --> 00:04:59.045
Every year for 100 years

00:04:59.045 --> 00:05:01.923
a different author is invited
to write something new.

00:05:02.382 --> 00:05:05.343
Their words will become
an anthology of books

00:05:05.343 --> 00:05:07.679
printed on paper made from these trees,

00:05:07.679 --> 00:05:10.640
only to be read in the year 2114.

00:05:11.766 --> 00:05:15.186
The core of Future Library
is time and longevity,

00:05:15.186 --> 00:05:17.188
but also hope and rituals.

00:05:17.981 --> 00:05:20.650
Every spring, we take
a pilgrimage to the forest.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.279
Authors hand over their manuscript
and announce its title.

00:05:24.863 --> 00:05:28.324
Their text will be stored here
in Oslo’s new library.

00:05:28.741 --> 00:05:30.493
You take off your shoes,

00:05:30.493 --> 00:05:31.661
you step inside.

00:05:32.245 --> 00:05:34.914
Each glass drawer holds a manuscript.

00:05:36.624 --> 00:05:40.044
100 years is not vast in cosmic terms.

00:05:40.378 --> 00:05:43.715
Yet if my young son
lives to read the books,

00:05:43.715 --> 00:05:47.427
his world is likely to have changed
beyond recognition.

00:05:47.886 --> 00:05:50.638
Will human beings survive
to read the books?

00:05:51.306 --> 00:05:54.183
Future Library is a century-long prayer.

00:05:54.851 --> 00:05:57.687
Future generations
may be invisible to our eyes,

00:05:57.687 --> 00:06:00.315
but we are connected through our actions.

00:06:01.149 --> 00:06:03.067
I wanted to see to future readers,

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:04.110
“I see you.”

00:06:04.527 --> 00:06:05.570
“We see you.”

00:06:06.487 --> 00:06:09.699
Why does connecting to deep time
matter to us all?

00:06:10.491 --> 00:06:14.704
Shortsightedness may be the greatest
threat to humanity.

00:06:15.163 --> 00:06:19.000
To be human is to understand
that we’re part of a long continuum.

00:06:19.626 --> 00:06:22.086
Let’s embrace our cosmic context,

00:06:22.086 --> 00:06:24.172
respect our origins

00:06:24.172 --> 00:06:26.382
and hold our future close.

00:06:27.258 --> 00:06:28.259
Thank you.

